    "rich_text_xbFPbM": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_ynjcCq": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<strong>Why Cats Love Pop Up Tents and Tubes</strong>",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text_faVBT6": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The psychology is simple: cats feel safe in enclosed spaces. Tents and tubes give them that plus an element of play.</p><ul><li><strong>Instinctual appeal:</strong> Cats are both predators and prey animals. Having a tunnel or enclosed tent satisfies their urge to hide while keeping watch.<br/><br/></li><li><strong>Stress relief:</strong> In busy households, shy or anxious cats often retreat to these structures to decompress.<br/><br/></li><li><strong>Exercise and play:</strong> Tubes add another layer—cats dash in and out, chase toys through the tunnel, and even ambush their feline housemates.<br/><br/></li></ul><p>When I brought home a fabric cat tube for my two young rescues, it quickly became their favorite toy. They spent hours chasing each other through it, then curling up together for a nap inside. That mix of fun and comfort is what makes these products stand out.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_ynjcCq",
        "text_faVBT6"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.rich-text.presets.name",
      "settings": {
        "desktop_content_position": "center",
        "content_alignment": "center",
        "color_scheme": "",
        "full_width": true,
        "padding_top": 40,
        "padding_bottom": 52
      }
    },
    "rich_text_KaQxnw": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_rXpewP": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<strong>Key Features That Matter in a Cat Pop Up Tent or Tube</strong>",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text_zht9aM": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<h3><strong>1. Portability and Storage</strong></h3><p>The biggest selling point is right in the name: <strong>pop up</strong>. These tents and tubes fold flat for storage and spring open in seconds. If you live in an apartment or travel often, this feature is a lifesaver. The Snarky Pets Cat Tube, for example, folds into a compact carrying size, making it easy to stash in a closet or take to a friend’s house when introducing your cat to a new space.</p><h3><strong>2. Durability</strong></h3><p>Cats don’t exactly go easy on their toys. Look for <strong>scratch-resistant mesh panels</strong> or durable polyester fabric. Reinforced seams help prevent tears when your cat claws at the entrance or pounces mid-chase. In my experience, cheaper tubes collapse or fray quickly, while sturdier options last for years with regular play.</p><h3><strong>3. Ventilation and Visibility</strong></h3><p>Mesh windows or breathable panels are important. They prevent overheating and give cats a sense of security while still letting them peek outside. For owners, visibility means you can keep an eye on what your cat is doing—especially useful if you have multiple cats playing together.</p><h3><strong>4. Size and Space</strong></h3><p>Check dimensions carefully. A <strong>cat pop up tent</strong> often provides more lounging space, while a <strong>cat tube</strong> gives active cats an outlet for running and hiding. Larger cats like Maine Coons need wide entrances (8–10 inches) to move comfortably.</p><h3><strong>5. Easy Cleaning</strong></h3><p>Accidents happen, especially if you take the tube or tent on the road. Choose designs with <strong>removable cushions, wipe-clean surfaces, or washable mats</strong>. It saves you from having a product that’s cute but unusable after a few spills.<br/><br/></p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_rXpewP",
        "text_zht9aM"
      ],
